Myth: Before the first song warmed the dark, the world was sealed inside a blue-black shell. The people lived by starlight, sharing cold roots and listening to the silence press against their doors. Above them flew Aven, the sparrow with one golden feather, searching for a fire that could sing.

Deep in the youngest mountain, Naro, she heard a humming ember. The older peaks had mocked him for being small, so he had hidden his voice in stone. “Little bird,” Naro rumbled, “carry this sun-seed away. My hands are rock, but yours are sky.”

Aven took the ember in her beak. At once the Sea rose jealous and pale. “All brightness must drown in me,” it thundered, throwing salt and shadow over her wings.

Aven faltered, but Naro lifted himself higher, cracking his roots, until his summit held her above the storm. There she opened her beak and sang. The sun-seed broke into sparks, and each spark remembered the song.

Some fell into caves and became hearths. Some fell into branches and became autumn leaves. One fell into the Sea, teaching waves to glitter.

That is why mountains blush at dawn, why birds sing before sunrise, and why every fire hums softly.
